#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-handoff — overlapping handoff: the successor starts ALONGSIDE its predecessor, reads the journal, asks
what the journal missed, and only then retires it. Nothing has to be idle for a session to start, so there is
nothing to defer on — which is what dissolves the "handoff PENDING, deferred again" loop.

  cc-handoff --overlap <target> [--pct N] [--session ID] [--in-flight "…"]   start a successor alongside
  cc-handoff --ready [<target>] [--forced]                run BY THE SUCCESSOR: preflight, commit, retire
  cc-handoff --replace <target> [--over [PCT]]            the OLD path: journal, exit, restart on fresh context
  cc-handoff --abandon <target> [--why "…"]               give up: successor's window goes, record clears
  cc-handoff --due <target> [--over-ceiling]              the deadline: below the ceiling abandon, at/over force
  cc-handoff --event <target> [--level L] [--pct N] [--session ID] [--in-flight "…"] [--live] [--taken]
                                    a context line was crossed (cc-context): decide and take the phase, print it
  cc-handoff --sweep                records no session is left to finish (run from the cc-reconcile timer)
  cc-handoff --kick <target> [<id>]         internal: brief the successor once it is up, and check it landed
  cc-handoff --retire <target>                            internal: DRAIN the predecessor, then end its window
  cc-handoff --relay                                      internal: from the UserPromptSubmit hook (cc-scope hook), the
                                                          hook payload on stdin — a prompt typed into the retiring
                                                          predecessor goes to its successor, blocked here with a pointer
  cc-handoff --brief <target>            ONE packet of everything a successor would otherwise re-read: the
                                         last journal entry, the unfinished board, what is landing, live
                                         workers, open PRs, open asks, and who the predecessor is
  cc-handoff --status [<target>] [--json]                 what phase, who is live, how long left
  cc-handoff selfcheck

ONE OWNER (2026-08-31, review rec #8). The handoff path used to be three files: `cc` held the stop-then-restart
sequence, `cc-context` decided which phase to take, and this file held the overlap. That split is what let a
survivor's un-gate depend on a marker nobody swept. Now `cc-context` MEASURES and emits one --event; every phase
decision, both paths and the sweep live here. `cc handoff <repo> <track>` delegates to --replace, and the overlap
still fails INTO it.

TARGET is `<repo>` (a planning session) or `<repo>/<track>` — the same address the Slack layer routes on, and
also the tmux window name in session `main`. The successor's window is `<target>~next` until cutover.

ONLY AN INTERACTIVE SESSION IS HANDED OFF. A headless cc-loop iteration (CC_ROLE=worker) takes no phase at all:
it journals, ends its turn, and the loop starts the next iteration on a fresh context reading that journal —
that IS its handoff, and it costs no second session. See `worker`.

THE INVARIANT. One record file, ~/.cc/state/handoff/<target>.json, names the LIVE session. The live one owns
the channel and may act on the world; the other is muted and gated (cc-guard reads the same record). Cutover
is ONE atomic os.replace() of that record — the single commit point — and everything after it is derived,
idempotent and retried, never rolled back. Ownership only ever moves forward, on an explicit successful act,
so every failure lands on "the predecessor is still in charge": a missing, corrupt or unreadable record means
the predecessor keeps the channel and the successor stays gated.

BOUNDED. deadline = started + CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX (default 45 min, ~/.cc/config). The watchdog is the
predecessor's own Stop hook, which already runs every turn — no new timer, no new daemon. At the deadline the
context ceiling decides: below it, abandon and tell the owner once; at or over it, force the cutover, or fall
back to today's stop-then-restart `cc handoff`. The new path fails INTO the old one and cannot take it down.

NOTHING IS LEFT TO A SESSION THAT MAY BE GONE. Every phase above is driven by a session's own Stop hook, which
is exactly what a crashed one no longer has — so --sweep, on the EXISTING cc-reconcile timer, finishes what no
session can: a record whose predecessor is gone past the grace is finalized (the marker cc-guard reads, then the
record), and an overlap that never reached --ready expires quietly past its deadline.

RETIREMENT DRAINS THE PREDECESSOR, THEN ENDS IT. A session cannot exit itself — `/exit` is a slash command a
human types into the TUI — so waiting for its pane to disappear waits for an event only a watching person can
cause. At cutover the NAMES move: the predecessor becomes `<target>~old` and the successor takes `<target>`, so
an owner attaching lands on the live one from the first instant. The predecessor then keeps its window for a
drain — CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN (600 s), or until it is idle with nothing typed, never less than
CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE (120 s: the turn in flight, the journal flushed) — and anything typed into it meanwhile
is RELAYED into the successor's window by the UserPromptSubmit hook (--relay) and blocked in place with a
pointer, so nothing typed is lost (2026-09-01 02:2xZ: the owner's question, typed a minute before the kill,
was). At drain end input is refused with the pointer and the window is KILLED — the drain expires into a kill,
never into "it keeps its window". A `~next` or `~old` window left with no record is reported by --sweep.

EVERY PROMPT THIS FILE TYPES INTO A SESSION LEADS WITH `[cc-handoff]` (MACHINE). A prompt a tool types reaches
UserPromptSubmit looking like a user turn, and the ask ledger opened a row for four of them — the successor's
opening prompt (a121, a126, a131) and the retiring session's stop message (a132) — each costing that session a
turn to decline a row about its own startup or retirement. The tag is the whole of what cc-scope matches on (its
NOT_A_PERSON), so it never learns a sentence and a prompt added here later is machine chatter from its first
use. A relay is NOT tagged: it carries a person's own words and must still open a row where it lands.

Env: CC_HANDOFF (the successor's own record id, exported into its process by `cc __runnext`; it OUTLIVES the
handoff, so `<id>.done` and not the variable is what says one is still open — mid_handoff) · CC_HANDOFF_WINDOW
(the successor's own tmux window, so it accepts its own startup dialog) ·
CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE, CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN, CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL, CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT (~/.cc/config) ·
CC_HANDOFF_DIR, CC_CTX_RECORDS (test hooks: put the record and the ledger somewhere else) ·
CC_BRIEF_WORKER_IDLE (--brief: how quiet a track's loop.log must be before it stops counting as live, 1 h).
"""
import fcntl, json, os, re, shlex, shutil, subprocess, sys, time, uuid

STATE = os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state")
DEV = os.path.expanduser("~/dev")        # where the checkouts are: --brief asks gh inside the target's own
HANDOFF = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_DIR") or os.path.join(STATE, "handoff")
RECORDS = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_RECORDS") or os.path.join(STATE, "context")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
SESSION = "main"
NEXT = "~next"                     # the successor's window suffix; `~` is not legal in a repo/track name
OLD = "~old"                       # ...and the predecessor's, from cutover to the end of its drain
DEFAULT_MAX = 45 * 60
TARGET_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}(/[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63})?$")
MACHINE = "[cc-handoff]"           # every prompt this file types into a session leads with it — see the header


def cfg(name, default):
    """A number from the environment, else ~/.cc/config — through cc-config, which applies that precedence
    for every tool on the box and never sources the file (it holds tokens)."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-config"), "get", name], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
        v = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        v = None
    try:
        return float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return default


def enabled():
    """`CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP=1` in ~/.cc/config turns the whole path on; the default is off, and flipping it is
    the owner's call. cc-slack mutes the non-live session since 2026-08-31 (delivery, the permission fanout
    and its own reply/react/file), so one channel keeps one voice while an overlap is open."""
    return cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP", 0) >= 1


# ---- the record ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def slug(target):
    return target.replace("/", "--")


def path(target):
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".json")


def read(target):
    """The open overlap for this target, or None. Missing and corrupt read the same on purpose: both mean
    'nobody has taken over', which is the direction every failure here must fall."""
    try:
        with open(path(target)) as f:
            r = json.load(f)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return r if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("id") and r.get("target") else None


def read_all():
    out = []
    for n in sorted(os.listdir(HANDOFF) if os.path.isdir(HANDOFF) else []):
        if not n.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(HANDOFF, n)) as f:
                r = json.load(f)
        except Exception:
            continue                      # one bad record never hides the rest
        if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("id") and r.get("target"):
            out.append(r)
    return out


def by_id(hid):
    for r in read_all():
        if r.get("id") == hid:
            return r
    return None


def write(rec):
    """THE COMMIT POINT. One os.replace(): every consumer sees either the whole old record or the whole new
    one, and there is no instant in which nobody is in charge."""
    os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
    p = path(rec["target"])
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(rec, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    return rec


def brief_path(target):
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".brief")


def clear(target):
    for f in (path(target), brief_path(target)):
        try:
            os.remove(f)
        except OSError:
            pass


ID_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$")       # a record id, and safe to build a filename from


def done_path(hid):
    """Where a COMPLETED retirement is remembered, keyed on the successor's own $CC_HANDOFF id."""
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, hid + ".done") if ID_RE.match(hid or "") else None


def mark_done(rec):
    """Evidence that the retirement FINISHED, and it must outlive clear(). The successor keeps $CC_HANDOFF for
    the life of its process (by design — the model must not be able to unset it), so without this a handoff
    that went RIGHT is indistinguishable from a record lost because something went WRONG, and cc-guard gates
    the only session left. On 2026-08-31 that bricked the session that won the first real handoff: it lost
    dispatch mid-sentence. Written BEFORE clear(), so there is no instant in which the record is gone and the
    reason it is gone is not on disk."""
    p = done_path(rec.get("id") or "")
    if not p:
        return False
    os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump({"id": rec["id"], "target": rec.get("target"), "retired": time.time()}, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    ttl = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL", 30 * 86400)   # once, not once per marker
    for n in sorted(os.listdir(HANDOFF)):          # no session outlives the TTL, so an older marker is litter
        if n.endswith(".done") and n != os.path.basename(p):
            q = os.path.join(HANDOFF, n)
            try:
                if os.path.getmtime(q) < time.time() - ttl:
                    os.remove(q)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return True


def mid_handoff():
    """Is this session the second half of a handoff that is STILL GOING ON? `cc __runnext` exports $CC_HANDOFF
    into a successor and it lives as long as that PROCESS — by design, so the model cannot unset it. So the
    variable answers "was this session BORN a successor", which is not the question anything here asks: once
    the handoff RETIRES, the successor is the only session left and must be able to hand ITSELF off in turn.
    Read as a bare flag it does the opposite — 2026-09-01: the planning session won an overlap at 03:06Z, ran
    all evening past the hand-off line to 51.5%, and no event ever fired, because it still carried
    CC_HANDOFF=bbbae9cd948f whose <id>.done had been on disk since 03:56Z. The overlap was a thing the box
    could do exactly ONCE per target, and the box's own handoff had to be started by hand.
    <id>.done is the same evidence cc-guard already un-gates on (cc-guard:84 — a record that is gone because
    everything went RIGHT vs one lost because something went WRONG); three files now read $CC_HANDOFF the same
    way. An id we cannot account for still holds: every failure here falls towards not policing your own."""
    hid = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF") or ""
    if not hid:
        return False
    return not (ID_RE.match(hid) and os.path.exists(os.path.join(HANDOFF, hid + ".done")))


def worker():
    """Is this turn a HEADLESS cc-loop iteration? Then it takes no phase: it journals, ENDS ITS TURN, and the
    loop starts the next iteration with a fresh context reading that journal — that IS the handoff, and it
    costs no second session. CC_ROLE=worker is the tell (cc-loop exports it around its `claude -p`, cc-secretary
    around its own, and a Stop hook inherits it — cc-scope reads the same variable the same way).
    The tmux window is NOT the tell, which is the whole bug: the LOOP holds the track's window while the session
    inside it is headless, so a worker read as `live`. On 2026-09-01 spend-tracker's iteration opened an overlap
    on that reading, and the INTERACTIVE successor it started finished the work, ran `cc done`, and then sat in
    the track window forever — the Home tally showed the track RUNNING over a stale board."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_ROLE") == "worker"


def live_side(rec):
    return "successor" if rec.get("live") == "successor" else "predecessor"


def ledger(rec, kind, why=""):
    """One countable line per phase — the handoff count stays a `wc -l`. `phases` in the record is what makes
    it once and not once per retry; an abandoned record is deleted, so a second --abandon is a no-op."""
    if kind in rec.setdefault("phases", []):
        return False
    rec["phases"].append(kind)
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    repo, _, track = rec["target"].partition("/")
    row = {"ts": time.time(), "kind": kind, "handoff": rec["id"], "target": rec["target"],
           "repo": repo, "track": track or None, "pct": rec.get("pct_at_start"),
           "session_id": (rec.get(live_side(rec)) or {}).get("session_id")}
    if why:
        row["why"] = why
    with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl"), "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")
    return True


# ---- tmux ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def tmux(*args, timeout=10):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tmux", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None


def win_id(name):
    """Address windows by id: names with `.` or `/` break `-t main:<name>`."""
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #W") or "").splitlines():
        wid, _, n = l.partition(" ")
        if n == name:
            return wid
    return None


def pane_live_id(wid):
    """A window whose wrapper shell has a child = a real session; a bare fallback shell has none."""
    if not wid:
        return False
    pids = (tmux("list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}") or "").split()
    if not pids:
        return False
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-P", pids[0]], capture_output=True).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def pane_live(name):
    return pane_live_id(win_id(name))


def side_live(rec, side):
    """Is that side's SESSION still there — asked BOTH ways, because either one alone says 'gone' too easily: by
    the window name it should hold, and by the window id the record names (a renamed window is still that
    session). The sweep un-gates on this answer, so a false 'gone' is the expensive direction."""
    s = rec.get(side) or {}
    return pane_live(s.get("window") or "") or pane_live_id(s.get("tmux") or "")


def win_alive(wid):
    """Is that window id still on the server? Ids are never reused, so this is the one safe way to ask."""
    return bool(wid) and wid in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") or "").split()


def side_wid(rec, side):
    """That side's window: by the name it should hold, else by the id the record names if it is still there.
    Both, because an expiry that finds neither leaves a successor nobody will ever finish (2026-09-01: six)."""
    s = rec.get(side) or {}
    return win_id(s.get("window") or "") or (s.get("tmux") if win_alive(s.get("tmux") or "") else None)


def age(pid):
    """Seconds since that pane's shell started — the window's age; tmux keeps no creation time."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "etimes=", "-p", str(pid)], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        return int(r.stdout.strip() or 0)
    except Exception:
        return 0


def orphans():
    """Windows named like a side of a handoff — `<target>~next`, `<target>~old` — whose target has NO record:
    nothing driven from a record will ever finish them. [(name, window id, age in s)], oldest first."""
    out = []
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #{pane_pid} #W") or "").splitlines():
        parts = l.split(" ", 2)
        if len(parts) < 3:
            continue
        wid, pid, name = parts
        for suf in (NEXT, OLD):
            if name.endswith(suf) and TARGET_RE.match(name[:-len(suf)]) and not read(name[:-len(suf)]):
                out.append((name, wid, age(pid)))
    return sorted(out, key=lambda x: -x[2])


def self_wid():
    """The window THIS process is running in, from $TMUX_PANE — never a bare `display-message`, which answers
    with whatever window is ACTIVE and would cheerfully name somebody else's session."""
    pane = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE") or ""
    if not pane:
        return ""
    return (tmux("display-message", "-p", "-t", pane, "#{window_id}") or "").strip()


def handoff_log():
    """Where a DETACHED child's one line goes. Resolved at CALL time, from $CC_CTX_RECORDS then $HOME — the
    two things a test redirects. It followed neither (it rebuilt the path from `~` at the moment of the call,
    and RECORDS was fixed at import), so a selftest's own `--retire` wrote _cctest lines into the box's REAL
    ~/.cc/state/context/handoff.log — 2026-09-01, found in it."""
    return os.path.join(os.environ.get("CC_CTX_RECORDS")
                        or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cc", "state", "context"), "handoff.log")


def run(argv, wait=True):
    try:
        if wait:
            return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60).returncode == 0
        log = handoff_log()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(log), exist_ok=True)   # a detached fallback that REFUSES must leave its one line somewhere
        out = open(log, "a")
        subprocess.Popen(argv, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=out, stderr=out, start_new_session=True)
        return True
    except Exception:
        return False


def notify(title, msg):
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-notify"), "-t", title, msg])


def alert(msg):
    """#alerts (owner, 2026-09-01): every handover leaves one line there. Best-effort like notify —
    a handover must never fail on Slack being down. Test state never pages: cc-notify's rule, which the
    Slack layer's post command does not apply — a synthetic _cctest/_selfcheck name, or CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY
    (every suite sets it), goes to the log file instead of #alerts: three _cctest handovers hit the REAL
    #alerts from inside a gate run the first night this shipped."""
    if os.environ.get("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY") or re.search(r"(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])_(cctest|selfcheck)", msg):
        try:
            with open(os.environ.get("CC_NOTIFY_LOG") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state/notify.log"), "a") as fh:
                fh.write(f"alerts\t{msg}\n")
        except OSError:
            pass
        return logd("not posted (test state): " + msg)
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-slack"), "post", "-c", "#alerts", msg])


# ---- phases --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BRIEF = """You are the SUCCESSOR in an overlapping handoff of {target}. Your FIRST tool call is \
`cc-handoff --brief {target}` — one packet holding the last entry of {journal} and the dated lines after it, \
the unfinished board, what is landing, the live workers, the open PRs and the open asks. That packet plus what \
your predecessor tells you is everything you start from; rebuilding it a call at a time is what made a handover \
cost about two million tokens. You are NOT the live session yet: the live one owns this target's channel and \
may act on the world, and cc-guard refuses merging, pushing, deploying, dispatching workers, `cc done` and \
outward posts from you until you take over. Read, search, think and ask — and NEVER ask your predecessor to run \
something you are denied. Your predecessor is still working in this same directory: it is the peer whose tmux \
id is {tmux} in `ListAgents`, so reach it with `SendMessage` (bidirectional, out of band from the channel) and \
ask it ONE batch of questions — everything the packet did not answer, in a single message, because it answers \
in one turn and each turn of its costs a whole context; subscribe with notify_when_idle, which is also how you \
hear if it exits. {flight} When you have the context and NOT BEFORE, run `cc-handoff --ready` — that single \
call is the commit point: it makes you live and retires your predecessor. If you cannot get there, say so in \
the journal; the overlap expires on its own and your predecessor simply carries on."""


def journal_for(target):
    repo, _, track = target.partition("/")
    return f"~/.cc/state/{repo}/{track}/progress.md" if track else f"~/.cc/state/{repo}/planning-handoff.md"


def logd(msg):
    """One line where a DETACHED child's output already goes — the only trace a process with no terminal leaves."""
    try:
        log = handoff_log()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(log), exist_ok=True)
        with open(log, "a") as f:
            f.write("%s cc-handoff: %s\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), msg))
    except Exception:
        pass


PROMPT_LINE = re.compile(r"^[^\w>❯]*[>❯](.*)$")   # Claude Code's `>` (`❯` since 2.1) — a taken turn, or the input box
DIALOG_MARKS = ("Do you want to", "No, and tell Claude", "Esc to cancel", "Enter to confirm",
                "Would you like to proceed?")    # cc-msg's dialog guard, the same strings: keys typed now answer a modal
IDLE_HINT = "? for shortcuts"                    # under the input box while the session waits for a turn
WORKING = "esc to interrupt"                     # the footer for the whole of a turn (cc-slack's WORKING_FOOTER)


def screen(wid, history=0):
    """What the pane shows now — and, with `history`, that many lines above it too. Dialogs are judged on the
    visible screen only: a dialog pressed through hours ago is still in the scrollback."""
    return tmux("capture-pane", "-p", *(("-S", str(-history)) if history else ()), "-t", wid) or ""


def dialog_open(wid):
    """Is a startup confirmation up in that pane — Claude Code's development-channels warning, or the MCP trust
    prompt? Keys typed while one is up go INTO IT, not into the session behind it."""
    return any(s in screen(wid) for s in ("Enter to confirm", "development channels", "local development",
                                          "Use this MCP server"))


def idle(wid):
    """Is the session in that pane WAITING, with nothing typed? No working footer, no dialog, and the input box —
    the bottom-most prompt line — empty: the TUI draws `❯ ` and a non-breaking space, older ones a placeholder.
    A blank pane is not idle: nothing has been drawn to be idle at."""
    pane = screen(wid)
    if not pane.strip() or WORKING in pane or any(s in pane for s in DIALOG_MARKS):
        return False
    last = None
    for l in pane.splitlines():
        m = PROMPT_LINE.match(l)
        if m:
            last = m.group(1)
    if last is None:
        return False
    last = last.replace("\u00a0", " ").strip()
    return not last or last.startswith('Try "')


def prompt_rendered(wid):
    """Has the session drawn its input box? The pane is live the instant the shell forks cc, long before Claude
    Code draws anything — liveness says nothing about whether keys will reach a session."""
    pane = screen(wid)
    return IDLE_HINT in pane or any(PROMPT_LINE.match(l) for l in pane.splitlines())


def box_holds(pane, text):
    """Is the text still SITTING in the input box — typed, Enter not taken? The box is the bottom-most `>` line
    on screen (a taken turn's echo sits above it); an idle box is empty or shows a placeholder, never the text."""
    last = None
    for l in pane.splitlines():
        m = PROMPT_LINE.match(l)
        if m:
            last = m.group(1)
    return last is not None and "".join(text.split())[:12] in "".join(last.split())


def landed(wid, text):
    """Did the prompt reach the SESSION as a turn — not the dialog on top of it, and not the input box alone?
    Read the pane back (scrollback too: a quick first reply pushes the echo up) and compare with ALL whitespace
    removed, because the TUI wraps a typed line at the pane width. An echo proves the keys arrived; the turn is
    proven by the working footer, or by the text having LEFT the input box (the 03:32Z successor sat with the
    brief typed and untaken at an empty prompt)."""
    pane = screen(wid, history=120)
    if "".join(text.split())[:48] not in "".join(pane.split()):
        return False
    return WORKING in pane or not box_holds(pane, text)


KICK = (MACHINE + "{hid}: you are the SUCCESSOR for {target} — your handoff brief is in your system prompt. "
        "Start now: run `cc-handoff --brief {target}` first, ask the predecessor ONE batch of questions about "
        "what the packet did not answer, and run `cc-handoff --ready` only once you have the context.")


def kick(target, hid=""):
    """--kick, detached by start(): put the opening prompt IN FRONT OF the successor, and CHECK IT LANDED.
    The brief rides in --append-system-prompt, which no session ever acts on by itself — with no first user
    turn a successor just sits there. On 2026-09-01 one sat blank for 25 minutes: the startup dialog ate the
    prompt typed at it, the window looked perfectly healthy, and nothing ever read the pane back. Liveness is
    no signal (the pane is live before anything is drawn), so type only once the dialog has shown AND cleared,
    or a prompt is on screen — pressing the dialog through from here too, because this path must not depend
    on the Slack layer being set up — then read the pane back, and keep sending until it is SEEN to land or
    the wait runs out; then tell the owner rather than leave a silent session sitting beside a live one."""
    win = target + NEXT
    wait = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT", 240)
    pause = 2 if wait else 0                       # a fixture sets the wait to 0 and gets no naps with it
    deadline = time.time() + wait
    text = KICK.format(hid=(" " + hid) if hid else "", target=target, journal=journal_for(target))
    seen = asked = False                           # seen: a dialog was on screen; asked: cc-slack is pressing it
    tries = 0
    while True:
        if not read(target):
            return 0                               # abandoned while we waited: nothing to brief
        wid = win_id(win)
        up = bool(wid) and pane_live(win)
        if up and dialog_open(wid):
            seen = True
            if not asked:
                run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-slack"), "accept-dialog", win], wait=False)
                asked = True                       # cc-slack watches that window for 3 min of its own accord
        elif up and (seen or tries or prompt_rendered(wid) or time.time() >= deadline):
            tries += 1                             # at the deadline with nothing recognisable drawn: one try, judged like any other
            run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), win, text])      # never types over a dialog: it answers a known-safe
                                                   # prompt, else spools (and collapses these retries into one line)
            for _ in range(5):                     # the TUI takes a moment to echo a turn
                time.sleep(pause)
                if landed(wid, text):
                    logd(f"{target}: the successor's opening prompt landed (try {tries})")
                    return 0
        if time.time() >= deadline:
            return kick_lost(target, f"its opening prompt did not land in {tries} tries" if tries
                             else "it never got past its startup dialog" if seen else "it never drew a prompt")
        time.sleep(pause)


def kick_lost(target, why):
    """A successor that never got its prompt is a session sitting silent beside a live one. Only the owner can
    unstick it, so it goes to #alerts and into the target's journal — the two places the next reader looks —
    and the record runs to its own deadline either way, so nothing is left half-owned."""
    line = (f"{target}: the successor came up but {why} — it is sitting blank in main:{target}{NEXT}; "
            f"brief it by hand (`cc-msg {target}{NEXT} …`) or `cc-handoff --abandon {target}`")
    logd("NO opening prompt — " + line)
    alert("⚠️ " + line)
    journal(target, line)
    return 1


def journal(target, line):
    """One dated line in the target's own journal — the file every successor reads first."""
    try:
        p = os.path.expanduser(journal_for(target))
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
        with open(p, "a") as f:
            f.write("\n## %s — cc-handoff\n%s\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%MZ", time.gmtime()), line))
    except Exception:
        pass


def start(target, pct=None, session_id="", flight=""):
    """--overlap: the successor comes up beside the predecessor, muted and gated, with the same working
    directory. Refused while a record for this target is open — one successor at a time, never a chain."""
    if not TARGET_RE.match(target):
        print(f"cc-handoff: '{target}' is not a target (<repo> or <repo>/<track>)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if worker():
        print("cc-handoff: a worker iteration is not handed off — it journals, ends its turn, and its loop "
              "starts the next one with a fresh context", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    old = read(target)
    if old:
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} already has an overlap open "
              f"(phase {old.get('phase')}, live {live_side(old)}) — one successor at a time")
        return 1
    wid = win_id(target)
    if not wid:
        print(f"cc-handoff: no live window main:{target} — nothing to start alongside")
        return 1
    if win_id(target + NEXT):
        print(f"cc-handoff: main:{target}{NEXT} already exists with no record — clear it by hand first")
        return 1
    now = time.time()
    hid = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]
    sid = str(uuid.uuid4())
    rec = {"id": hid, "target": target, "phase": "overlap", "live": "predecessor",
           "predecessor": {"session_id": session_id or "", "tmux": wid, "window": target},
           "successor": {"session_id": sid, "tmux": "", "window": target + NEXT},
           "started": now, "deadline": now + cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX", DEFAULT_MAX),
           "pct_at_start": pct, "told_owner": None, "phases": []}
    ledger(rec, "overlap-started")
    write(rec)          # before the window, never after: a successor whose record is missing must find itself gated
    with open(brief_path(target), "w") as f:
        f.write(BRIEF.format(target=target, journal=journal_for(target), tmux=wid,
                             flight=(f"Unfinished RIGHT NOW, which a journal always omits: {flight} — ask about it."
                                     if flight else "")))
    # The successor's OWN window name, carried into it: `cc __runnext` knows the target but not this suffix, so
    # it was accepting the startup dialog in the PREDECESSOR's window and its own sat on the development-channels
    # confirmation until a human noticed (2026-09-01 02:10Z, the third time this shape of bug has parked a session).
    cmd = "CC_HANDOFF_WINDOW=%s " % shlex.quote(target + NEXT)
    for v in ("CC_CLAUDE", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG", "CC_HANDOFF_DIR", "CC_CTX_RECORDS"):
        if os.environ.get(v):                     # the new window has the tmux server's env, not ours
            cmd += "%s=%s " % (v, shlex.quote(os.environ[v]))
    # `exec`: the pane's shell BECOMES cc, so claude is the pane's direct child — the one place cc-msg's
    # `pgrep -P` looks (forked, claude was a grandchild and every automatic kick was refused). __runnext's own
    # tail already ends in `exec bash`, so nothing is lost.
    cmd += "exec %s __runnext %s %s %s" % (shlex.quote(os.path.join(BIN, "cc")), shlex.quote(target), hid, sid)
    if tmux("new-window", "-d", "-t", SESSION, "-n", target + NEXT, cmd) is None:
        clear(target)                             # nothing came up: leave no record claiming there is a successor
        print(f"cc-handoff: could not start main:{target}{NEXT} — no overlap opened", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    rec["successor"]["tmux"] = win_id(target + NEXT) or ""
    write(rec)
    # Detached, because the successor takes a minute to boot and this call is on a Stop hook: it waits out the
    # startup dialog, sends the opening prompt and verifies the pane took it. Without it the successor has a
    # brief and no first turn — a window that looks right and a session doing nothing.
    if os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK"):   # a suite's fixture window never boots a claude: a kick there can only
        logd(f"kick skipped for {target} (CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK)")   # end in kick_lost, which pages — and tests must not
    else:
        run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--kick", target, hid], wait=False)
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — successor started in main:{target}{NEXT} (id {hid}); "
          f"it is muted and gated until it runs `cc-handoff --ready`")
    return 0


def ready(target=None, forced=False):
    """--ready: run BY THE SUCCESSOR, because the session that knows whether the transfer worked is the one
    that received it. Preflight, then the single os.replace(), then derive."""
    hid = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF") or ""
    rec = read(target) if target else (by_id(hid) if hid else None)
    if not rec:
        print(f"cc-handoff: no open overlap for {target or 'this session'}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    target = rec["target"]
    if live_side(rec) == "successor":
        derive(rec)                                # idempotent: a retried --ready re-derives, never rolls back
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} already cut over")
        return 0
    if not forced and hid != rec["id"]:
        print("cc-handoff: --ready belongs to the successor (its CC_HANDOFF must match the record); "
              "the predecessor's watchdog uses --forced", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if forced and not pane_live(rec["successor"]["window"]):
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} has no live successor to force onto", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    rec["live"] = "successor"                      # ---- THE COMMIT POINT ----
    rec["phase"] = "cutover"
    rec["cutover"] = time.time()
    alert(f"🔁 {target}: handover — the successor is live; the predecessor is retiring")
    ledger(rec, "overlap-forced" if forced else "overlap-cutover")
    write(rec)
    derive(rec)
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the successor is live; the predecessor is journalling, then retiring")
    return 0


def derive(rec):
    """Everything after the commit point: idempotent, retried, never rolled back. A failure here leaves the
    record right about WHO IS LIVE and wrong only about cosmetics, and a later turn re-derives the same state."""
    target = rec["target"]
    repo, _, track = target.partition("/")
    sid = (rec.get("successor") or {}).get("session_id")
    if track and sid:
        board = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board")
        run([board, "set", repo, track, "session_id", sid])
        run([board, "set", repo, track, "started", "true"])
        run([board, "note", repo, track, "handoff: the overlapping successor took over"])
    # THE NAMES MOVE NOW, not at the end of the drain: an owner attaching to main:<target>, and a `cc-msg <target>`,
    # must reach the live session from the first instant after the commit point. By id — a window renamed by
    # hand is still that session — and the predecessor first, which is what frees the name.
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    suc = (rec.get("successor") or {}).get("tmux") or win_id(target + NEXT) or ""   # no id on the record: by its startup name
    old = target + OLD
    # ONE tmux call for both: between two, no window is `<target>`, and a `cc <repo>` in that instant starts a third session.
    cmds = ["rename-window", "-t", pre, old] if win_alive(pre) else []
    if win_alive(suc):
        cmds += ([";"] if cmds else []) + ["rename-window", "-t", suc, target]
    if cmds:
        tmux(*cmds)
    # kick() spooled the opening prompt under the successor's STARTUP name (<target>~next); the rename above would
    # strand that mail forever (review-157b) — it moves with the window, appended behind anything already spooled
    # for the live name, and the ~next state dir (only ever cc-msg inbox files; `~` is not legal in a name) goes too.
    nxt = os.path.join(STATE, target + NEXT)
    nsp = os.path.join(nxt, "inbox.spool")
    if os.path.exists(nsp):
        tdir = os.path.join(STATE, target)
        os.makedirs(tdir, exist_ok=True)
        # UNDER CC-MSG'S OWN LOCK. Its 60 s drain rewrites <target>/inbox.spool as `tail -n +2 > tmp && mv`, so an
        # append landing between that tail and its mv is replaced by the mv — and the rmtree below then deletes the
        # only other copy, which is the exact stranding this hunk exists to prevent. Same file, same .inbox.lock.
        with open(os.path.join(tdir, ".inbox.lock"), "w") as lk:
            fcntl.flock(lk, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            with open(nsp) as src, open(os.path.join(tdir, "inbox.spool"), "a") as dst:
                dst.write(src.read())
            shutil.rmtree(nxt, ignore_errors=True)     # inside the lock: nothing is deleted that is not yet copied
    shutil.rmtree(nxt, ignore_errors=True)             # …and the ~next dir goes whether or not it held mail
    if (rec["predecessor"].get("window"), rec["successor"].get("window"), rec["successor"].get("tmux")) != (old, target, suc):
        rec["predecessor"]["window"], rec["successor"]["window"], rec["successor"]["tmux"] = old, target, suc
        write(rec)
    if pane_live_id(pre):
        run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), old, RETIRE_MSG.format(target=target, old=old)])
    run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--retire", target], wait=False)


RETIRE_MSG = (MACHINE + " Handoff complete: your successor is live and owns this target now — it holds window "
              "{target}; you are {old} and retiring. Append your final entry to the journal — what is done, what "
              "is next (ordered), gotchas — and stop. You are no longer the live session: merging, pushing, "
              "dispatching and posting outward are refused from here on, anything typed here is relayed to your "
              "successor, and this window ends by itself once you are idle.")
RELAY_PREFIX = "[relayed from the retiring session] "
# What reaches UserPromptSubmit as a "user" turn but was written by the harness or a peer — cc-scope's
# NOT_A_PERSON less MACHINE, which is this file's own tag and is guarded beside this list; kept in step by hand. Relayed, the successor's own cross-session message came back typed into it, and the
# predecessor lost its task notifications mid-journal (review of #140). A <channel …> Slack turn is NOT here: that
# is a person, and the one the drain exists for.
NOT_A_PERSON = ("<task-notification", "<cross-session-message", "[SYSTEM NOTIFICATION",
                "[Cross-session idle notice]", "<command-name", "<local-command-stdout")


def retiring(payload):
    """The record under which THIS process's session is the retired predecessor, or None. Keyed on the window —
    $TMUX_PANE's window id against the id the record names — and, second, on the payload's session_id, which
    the record may hold EMPTY (a planning session's is unknown at --overlap time: the 2026-09-01 incident's was),
    so the window is the key that always works. Never a guess: no match, no record."""
    recs = read_all()
    if not recs:
        return None
    wid, sid = self_wid(), payload.get("session_id") or ""
    for rec in recs:
        if live_side(rec) != "successor":
            continue                               # before cutover nobody is retiring, whatever the window
        pre = rec.get("predecessor") or {}
        if (wid and wid == pre.get("tmux")) or (sid and sid == pre.get("session_id")):
            return rec
    return None


def block(reason):
    print(json.dumps({"decision": "block", "reason": reason}))
    return 0


def relay(payload):
    """--relay, called by the UserPromptSubmit hook (`cc-scope hook`) with the hook's payload on stdin. Prints a
    block decision when the prompt was typed into the RETIRING predecessor: during the drain it is relayed into
    the successor's window (cc-msg <target>) and blocked here with a pointer; once the drain is over (phase
    `retired`) it is refused with the pointer. Prints NOTHING — the hook then does nothing — in every other
    case: another session, a slash command (that is for THIS TUI: /exit is how a human ends it), a turn the harness
    or a peer wrote (NOT_A_PERSON), a prompt this file typed itself (MACHINE), and a relay cc-msg could not take at
    all (no window, no interactive claude there): then the prompt stays with the session it was typed into, which
    is alive and answers it, and the journal keeps a copy. A relay cc-msg QUEUED (the successor's pane is busy or
    holds a dialog) counts as relayed — its drain delivers it. Nothing typed is ever lost."""
    text = payload.get("prompt") or ""
    if not text.strip():
        return 0
    rec = retiring(payload)
    if not rec:
        return 0
    target = rec["target"]
    if text.lstrip().startswith(("/", MACHINE) + NOT_A_PERSON):
        return 0                                   # whatever the phase: /exit must still end a retired TUI
    if rec.get("phase") == "retired":
        return block(f"retired — use window {target}")
    line = RELAY_PREFIX + " ".join(text.splitlines())   # one line: a newline typed into the TUI would submit early
    if run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), target, line]):
        logd(f"{target}: relayed a prompt from the retiring session ({len(text)} chars)")
        return block(f"relayed to {target} — this session is retiring; the answer will come from window {target}")
    journal(target, f"NOT relayed (cc-msg could not reach main:{target}); the retiring session answers it: {text}")
    logd(f"{target}: could not relay a prompt from the retiring session — it answers it itself")
    return 0


def refuse_kill(rec, wid):
    """THE ONE GENUINELY DANGEROUS ACT IN THIS FILE. Killing the wrong window ends a working session and loses
    a context nobody can get back, so the kill is fenced by three refusals and each says which one it was.
    A caller that gets a reason back must kill NOTHING."""
    if not wid:
        return "there is no window left to end"
    if wid != ((rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""):
        return (f"tmux window {wid} is not the one the record names as the predecessor "
                f"({(rec.get('predecessor') or {}).get('tmux') or 'none'})")
    if wid == ((rec.get("successor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""):
        return "the record names the same window on both sides"
    if wid == self_wid():
        return "that is the window --retire is running in"
    return ""


def retire(target):
    """--retire, detached by derive(): DRAIN the predecessor, then END it — every time, not only when a human
    happened to be watching. The old wait-it-out never completed on its own (a session cannot exit itself; on
    2026-08-31 a retired one ran on for 35 min as a second live voice), and the plain grace-then-kill that
    replaced it lost what was typed in its last minute (2026-09-01 02:2xZ). So the window lives on — renamed
    `<target>~old` at cutover — for CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN, or until it is idle with nothing typed, never less than
    CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE (the turn in flight, the journal flushed), while --relay carries anything typed into
    it to the successor. Then the record says `retired` (--relay refuses from here) and the window is killed.
    Kills ONLY the window the record names as the predecessor, ONLY once the record says the successor is live,
    and never the caller's own — see refuse_kill(). No record, or no cutover: nothing is killed at all."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
        return 0                                   # missing, corrupt, or before cutover: kill NOTHING
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    grace, drain = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE", 120), cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN", 600)
    t0 = rec.get("cutover") or time.time()
    quiet = 0
    while pane_live_id(pre):                       # a session that exited leaves a bare shell: nothing left to drain
        now = time.time()
        if now >= t0 + drain:
            break
        quiet = quiet + 1 if now >= t0 + grace and idle(pre) else 0
        if quiet >= 2:                             # two reads apart: one idle glimpse mid-typing is not "nothing queued"
            break
        time.sleep(5)
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
        return 0                                   # finalized or cleared under us: nothing here is ours to end
    if rec.get("phase") != "retired":
        rec["phase"] = "retired"                   # the drain is over: from here --relay REFUSES with the pointer
        write(rec)
    wid = pre if win_alive(pre) else ""            # by the id the record names: `<target>` is the SUCCESSOR's name now
    if wid:
        why = refuse_kill(rec, wid)
        if why:                                    # the record and tmux disagree: the record stays, and so does the gate
            print(f"cc-handoff: {target}'s predecessor was NOT ended — {why}. It keeps its window and "
                  f"everything stays gated; this needs a hand.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    nid = win_id(target + NEXT)                    # a successor still on its startup name: a cutover older than this code
    if nid and not win_id(target):
        tmux("rename-window", "-t", nid, target)
    # mail addressed to the retired window dies with it: RETIRE_MSG may have spooled under <target>~old, and a
    # spool whose window is gone would otherwise stand as junk and be reported as stranded (review-157b)
    shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(STATE, target + OLD), ignore_errors=True)
    rec["phase"] = "done"
    ledger(rec, "overlap-retired")
    write(rec)
    mark_done(rec)                                 # before clear(), always: it is what tells cc-guard this went RIGHT
    clear(target)                                  # nobody is gated any more: the successor is the only session left
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the predecessor is ended, the successor has its window, nothing is gated")
    return 0


def abandon(target, why=""):
    """--abandon: the deadline below the ceiling. The successor's window goes, the record clears, the owner
    hears it ONCE — the record's absence is what makes a second call a no-op. Never two sessions forever."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec:
        return 0
    if live_side(rec) == "successor":
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} has already cut over — nothing to abandon", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    wid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
    if wid:
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    rec["told_owner"] = time.time()
    ledger(rec, "overlap-abandoned", why)
    clear(target)
    notify(target, f"Overlapping handoff abandoned for {target}: {why or 'the successor never became ready'}. "
                   f"The original session is still running and still in charge.")
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — overlap abandoned, the predecessor carries on")
    return 0


def due(target, over_ceiling=False):
    """--due: the predecessor's Stop hook, every turn. Silent until the deadline; then the ceiling decides."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or live_side(rec) == "successor" or time.time() < rec.get("deadline", 0):
        return 0
    if not over_ceiling:
        return abandon(target, "the successor did not become ready inside the overlap window")
    if pane_live(rec["successor"]["window"]):
        return ready(target, forced=True)          # it has the journal, which is all a fresh session ever got
    _, _, track = target.partition("/")
    abandon(target, "at the context ceiling with no live successor")
    if track:                                      # fall back INTO the old path: stop, journal, restart fresh
        run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--replace", target], wait=False)
    else:
        notify(target, f"{target} is at its context ceiling and the overlapping handoff did not take. "
                       f"There is no stop-then-restart path for a planning session — it needs a hand.")
    return 0


REPLACE_MSG = (MACHINE + " Handoff: append to {jrn} a dated handoff entry for a fresh session — what is done, "
               "what is next (ordered), gotchas/decisions — then run /exit.")


def replace(target, over=None):
    """--replace: the ORIGINAL handoff, and the one the overlap falls back INTO — tell the session to journal,
    wait for it to exit, then restart it on a fresh context. It was `cc handoff <repo> <track>` until
    2026-08-31; `cc` still spells it that way and delegates here, so the lifecycle has one owner and one file
    to read. Every refusal leaves the session running and says why: a restart that loses a context nobody
    journalled is worse than no restart.
    A handoff costs the session its working memory, so --over decides on the MEASURED number, never a hunch."""
    repo, _, track = target.partition("/")
    if not track:
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} is a planning session — there is no stop-then-restart path for one",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not win_id(target):
        print(f"cc-handoff: no live window {target}")
        return 1
    st = os.path.join(STATE, repo, track)
    jrn = os.path.join(st, "progress.md")
    if over:
        argv = [os.path.join(BIN, "cc-context"), repo, track, "--over"] + ([] if over == "any" else [over])
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
            rc, ctx = r.returncode, r.stdout.split("  ")[0].strip()
        except Exception:
            rc, ctx = 2, ""
        line = "under the handoff line" if over == "any" else f"under {over}%"
        if rc == 2:
            print(f"cc-handoff: cannot measure {target}'s context (no answered turn yet) — "
                  f"not handing off on a guess")
            return 1
        if rc == 1:
            print(f"cc-handoff: {target} is at {ctx}, {line} — nothing to hand off")
            return 0
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} is at {ctx} (over {line[len('under '):]}) — handing off")
    else:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-context"), repo, track],
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
            if r.returncode == 0:
                print(f"cc-handoff: {target} is at {r.stdout.split('  ')[0].strip()} — handing off")
        except Exception:
            pass
    def mtime():
        try:
            return os.stat(jrn).st_mtime
        except OSError:
            return 0
    before = mtime()
    if not run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), target, REPLACE_MSG.format(jrn=jrn)]):
        print(f"cc-handoff: cc-msg could not reach {target} (no window, or no interactive session there) — "
              f"not restarting", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    for _ in range(120):
        if not pane_live(target):
            break
        time.sleep(5)
    if pane_live(target):
        print("cc-handoff: session did not exit in 10 min; the handoff journal may still be written. "
              "Not restarting.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if mtime() == before:
        print(f"cc-handoff: {jrn} never changed — no handoff journal was written, so a fresh context would "
              f"start blind. Not restarting (read the pane, then: cc {repo} {track})", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    wid = win_id(target)
    if wid:
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    board = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board")
    run([board, "set", repo, track, "session_id", str(uuid.uuid4())])
    run([board, "set", repo, track, "started", "false"])
    run([board, "note", repo, track, "handoff: restarted with fresh context"])
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc"), repo, track])
    return 0


def event(target, level="", pct=None, session="", busy="", live=False, taken=False):
    """--event: the ONE call cc-context makes. It measures and says WHAT was crossed; which phase that means is
    decided here, in the file that owns every phase. Returns the action taken, for the caller's own record:
      ""         nothing — the line was the journal nudge, or a phase is already open, or nothing could start
      "overlap"  a successor is coming up alongside (the predecessor is not interrupted)
      "replace"  the old stop-then-restart path is running, detached
    `taken` = that session has already taken a phase, or a Stop hook is holding it: nothing NEW starts, but the
    level still decides the watchdog below, which is why cc-context sends it either way.
    Never from the SUCCESSOR of an OPEN handoff: a session must not police, abandon or restart the handoff it
    is itself the second half of — but only while that handoff is still open (mid_handoff).
    Never for a headless cc-loop iteration either (worker): its loop is its handoff, and there is nothing here
    that is worth a second session. The level is still MEASURED and the session still told to journal — that is
    cc-context's half — but no phase is opened, so the watchdog below is not run for it."""
    if worker() or mid_handoff():
        return ""
    if read(target):
        due(target, level == "ceiling")     # an overlap is open: its deadline, every turn, from the predecessor
    if taken or not level or level == "journal" or not live or read(target):
        return ""                           # the journal line is a nudge, not a phase; and one phase at a time
    if enabled() and start(target, pct, session, busy) == 0:
        return "overlap"                    # nothing has to be idle: starting a second session interrupts nobody
    if "/" in target and not busy:          # the overlap is off or would not open: fail INTO the old path, which
        run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--replace", target], wait=False)   # DOES have to wait for idle
        return "replace"
    return ""


def finalize(rec, why):
    """A retirement nobody is left to finish. mark_done() BEFORE clear(), always: that order is the only reason
    the survivor of a completed handoff is not gated by the record of its own success."""
    target = rec["target"]
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    # The predecessor's SESSION is gone; its window may not be — a session that exited leaves a bare shell that
    # still holds `<target>`, so the rename below never happened and six successors sat on as `~next` with no
    # record (2026-09-01 05:15Z). An empty shell is not a session: it goes, under the same fence as a kill.
    if win_alive(pre) and not pane_live_id(pre) and not refuse_kill(rec, pre):
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", pre)
    nid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
    if nid and not win_id(target):                 # the predecessor is gone: its name is free
        tmux("rename-window", "-t", nid, target)
    rec["phase"] = "done"
    alert(f"🔁 {target}: handover finalized by the sweep — {why}")
    ledger(rec, "overlap-swept", why)
    write(rec)
    mark_done(rec)
    clear(target)
    repo, _, track = target.partition("/")
    if track:
        run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board"), "note", repo, track, f"handoff: swept — {why}"])
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — {why}; the record is finalized and nothing is gated by it")


def sweep():
    """--sweep: the reaper, run from the EXISTING cc-reconcile timer (no new unit). Everything else here is
    driven by a session's own Stop hook, which is precisely what a crashed session no longer has: a retirement
    that dies between mark_done() and clear() leaves the survivor gated by the record of its own success, and a
    predecessor that dies mid-overlap leaves a record nothing reaps. Two cases, both unambiguous:
      the predecessor's session is GONE past the retire grace -> finalize. Nobody but the successor is in
        charge of that target, and a record saying otherwise is gating the only session left.
      an overlap that never reached --ready is past its deadline -> it expires QUIETLY. The predecessor never
        stopped working and was never told anything, so there is nothing to tell it now; --due, which the
        predecessor's own hook runs, gets first refusal (hence the extra grace) and tells the owner when it acts.
      a cut-over predecessor STILL ALIVE past drain+grace -> ended here, then finalized. The --retire that should
        have ended it died, or refused because a --forced cutover runs it from the predecessor's own pane
        (refuse_kill); left alone it is `~old` forever at phase retired — prompts refused, not relayed — and its
        successor can never hand off (mid_handoff). Same fence as --retire; the sweep has no pane of its own.
    A sweep only ever un-gates, so a WRONG 'gone' is the expensive direction: if tmux cannot be read at all —
    no server, no session main — every window looks gone, so nothing is swept."""
    if tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") is None:
        return 0
    grace, drain, now = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE", 120), cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN", 600), time.time()
    for rec in read_all():
        target = rec["target"]
        if not TARGET_RE.match(target):
            continue                               # a record naming something that is not a target: not ours to act on
        pred_live = side_live(rec, "predecessor")
        if pred_live and rec.get("gone_since"):
            rec.pop("gone_since", None)            # it came back: a misread, which is exactly what the stamp absorbs
            write(rec)
        if not pred_live and now - (rec.get("cutover") or rec.get("started") or 0) > grace:
            # TWO STRIKES, a sweep apart. One transient tmux/pgrep misread here would un-gate the successor while
            # the predecessor keeps working — two un-gated voices, the 2026-08-31 incident class. The first sweep
            # that finds nobody only writes down WHEN; the finalize needs a later sweep still finding nobody.
            gone = rec.get("gone_since") or 0
            if not gone:
                rec["gone_since"] = now
                write(rec)
            elif now - gone >= cfg("CC_HANDOFF_GONE_CONFIRM", 300):
                finalize(rec, "the predecessor's session is gone (two sweeps apart)")
        elif live_side(rec) == "successor" and now - (rec.get("cutover") or 0) > drain + grace:
            rec = read(target)                     # the DECIDING read, as below
            if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
                continue
            pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
            why = refuse_kill(rec, pre)
            if why:
                print(f"cc-handoff: {target}'s predecessor outlived its drain and was NOT ended — {why}", file=sys.stderr)
                continue
            tmux("kill-window", "-t", pre)
            finalize(rec, "the predecessor outlived its drain (its --retire never ended it); ended by the sweep")
        elif live_side(rec) == "predecessor" and now > rec.get("deadline", 0) + grace:
            rec = read(target)                     # the DECIDING read: a --ready committing after the loop copied
            if not rec or live_side(rec) != "predecessor" or now <= rec.get("deadline", 0) + grace:
                continue                           # this record means the kill below would hit the LIVE successor
            wid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
            if wid:
                tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
            ledger(rec, "overlap-expired", "the overlap window passed with no --ready")
            clear(target)
            print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the overlap expired without a cutover; the predecessor carries on")
    # A `~next` or `~old` window with NO record is one nothing here will ever finish: reported, once per window,
    # not killed — a session with no record is exactly the case where nobody can say whose it is.
    told_p = os.path.join(HANDOFF, "orphans.told")
    try:
        told = json.load(open(told_p))
    except Exception:
        told = {}
    alive = set((tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") or "").split())
    told = {w: t for w, t in told.items() if w in alive}
    for name, wid, secs in orphans():
        if secs < cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX", DEFAULT_MAX) or wid in told:
            continue
        told[wid] = now
        line = (f"main:{name} has been up {secs / 3600:.1f} h with no handoff record — nobody will finish it: "
                f"brief it by hand or `tmux kill-window -t {wid}`")
        alert("⚠️ " + line)
        print("cc-handoff: " + line)
    try:
        os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
        with open(told_p + ".tmp", "w") as f:
            json.dump(told, f)
        os.replace(told_p + ".tmp", told_p)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return 0


def status(target=None, as_json=False):
    recs = [r for r in ([read(target)] if target else read_all()) if r]
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(recs))
        return 0 if recs else 1
    orph = [] if target else orphans()
    if not recs:
        print("no overlapping handoff is open" + (f" for {target}" if target else ""))
    for name, wid, secs in orph:
        print(f"{name:<24} orphan    no record — window {wid} up {secs / 3600:.1f} h: brief it by hand, "
              f"or tmux kill-window -t {wid}")
    if not recs:
        return 1
    now = time.time()
    for r in recs:
        left = int((r.get("deadline", 0) - now) / 60)
        alive = "alive" if pane_live(r["successor"]["window"]) else "gone"
        print(f"{r['target']:<24} {r.get('phase', '?'):<9} live={live_side(r):<11} "
              f"{left:>4}m left   successor main:{r['successor']['window']} ({alive})   id {r['id']}")
    return 0


# ---- the brief -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A handover measured on 2026-09-01 cost ~2M input-equivalent tokens: the successor made 71
# tool calls before `--ready` (6.0M cache reads) and the predecessor answered over 18 turns at a 400k context.
# Almost none of that was new information — it was the same journal, board, queue and PR list read again, one
# tool call at a time, by a session with an empty context. So the box assembles the packet ONCE, in the order
# a successor needs it, and the questions left over are the ones a file genuinely cannot answer.
# THE JOURNAL IS READ FROM THE END. A track journal is a running file; a planning one runs to hundreds of kB.
# The successor needs the last entry and the dated lines after it, and reading the rest is the expense this
# whole command exists to remove — so the file is read backwards, a page at a time, and stops at the entry it
# was after. TWO SHAPES, because the box writes two: a planning journal marks its handoff entries "journal at
# N%" and a track's `progress.md` does not (of the 93 here, none does) — so a missing marker is the NORMAL
# case for a track and must not tip the raw tail into the packet. The marker wins where there is one; where
# there is none the last dated `## ` heading is the entry, and the packet says which of the two it cut on.
BRIEF_CAP = 64 * 1024                # the most of a journal --brief will ever read
JOURNAL_MARK = re.compile(r"^## .*journal at \d+%", re.M)
JOURNAL_HEAD = re.compile(r"^## ", re.M)         # any dated entry: what a track journal has instead
ANSI = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")
BOARD_ROW = re.compile(r"^  \S+ (\S+) \[([a-z]+)\]")     # "  ▶ <track> [running]  <title>", ANSI stripped
BOARD_CONT = "      "                                     # its indented task/PR/note lines
DONE_ROWS = ("merged", "done")
LANE = re.compile(r"^lane[0-9]+$")                        # the tmux land-lane crutch (retired in #154, not gone)
PR_IN = re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])([0-9]{1,6})(?![0-9])")


def out(argv, timeout=25, cwd=None, fail=""):
    """A helper's stdout, or `fail`. A brief never fails: a section the box cannot fill says so and the rest
    is still worth the successor's one call. `fail` is for the sections where EMPTY and BROKEN read the same
    — no open PRs and a `gh` that would not run both print nothing, and only one of them is good news."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout,
                           cwd=cwd if cwd and os.path.isdir(cwd) else None)
        # ...and only where one was asked for: the other sections print whatever a helper managed to say,
        # which is what they did before this argument existed.
        return fail if (fail and r.returncode != 0) else (r.stdout or "")
    except Exception:
        return fail


def last_match(pat, text, pos):
    """The last match of `pat` in `text`, ignoring one at index 0 while bytes remain below it: `^` matches
    there too, and mid-file that is the middle of a line, not the start of one."""
    found = None
    for m in pat.finditer(text):
        if m.start() == 0 and pos > 0:
            continue
        found = m
    return found


def tail_entry(path, cap=BRIEF_CAP):
    """The last journal entry and everything after it, read BACKWARDS from the end of the file. Returns
    (text, bytes_read, anchor) — `anchor` names what it cut on, because the two journal shapes cut on
    different things and the packet says which; `bytes_read` is what the selfcheck pins: never the whole
    file, and never more than `cap`."""
    path = os.path.expanduser(path)
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(path)
    except OSError:
        return "", 0, "missing file"
    read = 0
    buf = b""
    pos = size
    text = ""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            while pos > 0 and read < cap:
                step = min(8192, pos, cap - read)
                pos -= step
                fh.seek(pos)
                buf = fh.read(step) + buf
                read += step
                text = buf.decode("utf-8", "replace")
                mark = last_match(JOURNAL_MARK, text, pos)
                if mark:
                    return text[mark.start():], read, "last 'journal at N%' entry"
                # NO EARLY FALLBACK. Two whole unmarked "## " entries in the buffer used to be enough to
                # stop here — but a planning journal runs marked entry, then two or three unmarked OVERLAP /
                # FINAL / TAKEOVER entries, so on any page but the last that test fires INSIDE a marked entry
                # and the packet starts below the very entry the successor is told to read (review of #181).
                # A track journal has no marker anywhere, and its fallback is the same one, after the loop.
    except OSError:
        return "", read, "unreadable file"
    head = last_match(JOURNAL_HEAD, text, pos)
    if head:
        return text[head.start():], read, "last dated entry"
    return (f"(no dated '## ' entry in the last {read // 1024} kB of {path} — the tail follows)\n"
            + buf.decode("utf-8", "replace"), read, "raw tail")


def board_open(repo):
    """`cc board show <repo>`, minus the rows that are finished — a successor inherits what is unfinished."""
    keep, drop = [], False
    for line in ANSI.sub("", out([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board"), "show", repo])).splitlines():
        m = BOARD_ROW.match(line)
        if m:
            drop = m.group(2) in DONE_ROWS
        elif not line.startswith(BOARD_CONT):
            drop = False                                  # the board's own header line: not a row at all
        if line.strip() and not drop:
            keep.append(line)
    return "\n".join(keep)


def lanes():
    """What is LANDING right now. Two shapes, because the box has both: a `lane<N>` tmux window (the crutch
    #154 retired — one left running is exactly the thing a successor must be told about) and the queue that
    replaced it, one job file per PR under ~/.cc/state/land/."""
    rows = []
    for line in (tmux("list-windows", "-a", "-F",
                      "#{window_name}\t#{window_id}\t#{pane_pid}\t#{pane_start_command}") or "").splitlines():
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if len(fields) == 4 and LANE.match(fields[0]):
            pr = PR_IN.search(fields[3].replace(fields[0], " "))
            rows.append(f"{fields[0]:<10} window {fields[1]:<6} PR {pr.group(1) if pr else '?':<6} waiter pid {fields[2]}")
    land = os.path.join(STATE, "land")
    try:
        jobs = sorted(f for f in os.listdir(land) if f.endswith(".json"))
    except OSError:
        jobs = []
    for j in jobs:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(land, j)) as fh:
                r = json.load(fh)
        except Exception:
            continue
        rows.append(f"queue      {r.get('repo', '?')} PR {r.get('pr', '?')}   stage {r.get('stage', '?')}   "
                    f"attempt {r.get('attempts', 0)}   queued {r.get('queued_at', '?')}")
    return "\n".join(rows)


def live_workers(repo):
    """One line per track whose loop is still running: the last line of its loop.log, which is where a worker
    says where it got to."""
    rows = []
    base = os.path.join(STATE, repo)
    try:
        tracks = sorted(os.listdir(base))
    except OSError:
        return ""
    idle = cfg("CC_BRIEF_WORKER_IDLE", 3600)              # once, not once per track: a repo has scores of them
    for t in tracks:
        log = os.path.join(base, t, "loop.log")
        try:
            if time.time() - os.path.getmtime(log) > idle:
                continue                                  # long finished: the board carries those
            last = ""
            for last in open(log, errors="replace"):
                pass
        except OSError:
            continue
        rows.append(f"{t:<24} {last.strip()[:160]}")
    return "\n".join(rows)


def prs(repo):
    """The TARGET's open PRs. `gh` resolves a repo from its cwd, and --brief is run from wherever the caller
    happens to be: from another repo's checkout it printed that repo's PRs under this one's heading, and from
    a dir that is no checkout at all it printed none — which a successor reads as "nothing is open". So it is
    asked inside the target's own checkout, and a gh that could not answer says so rather than showing empty."""
    where = os.path.join(DEV, repo)          # DEV is already absolute; the selfcheck points it at its own
    if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(where, ".git")):
        return f"(unavailable: {where} is not a checkout, so gh has no repo to list)"
    return out(["gh", "pr", "list", "--limit", "40", "--json", "number,headRefName,mergeable", "--template",
                "{{range .}}{{printf \"%-6v %-38v %v\\n\" .number .headRefName .mergeable}}{{end}}"],
               cwd=where, fail=f"(unavailable: gh could not list the open PRs of {repo})")


def brief(target):
    """--brief: ONE packet, in the order a successor needs it, so it does not rebuild it a tool call at a
    time. Read-only and best-effort throughout — this must never be the thing that fails a handoff."""
    repo = target.partition("/")[0]
    rec = read(target)
    journal, jread, janchor = tail_entry(journal_for(target))
    pre = (rec or {}).get("predecessor") or {}
    who = [f"target        {target}",
           f"predecessor   window main:{pre.get('window') or target}   tmux {pre.get('tmux') or win_id(target) or '?'}",
           f"              session {(pre.get('session_id') or '?')}   (SendMessage/ListAgents match on the tmux id)"]
    if rec:
        who.append(f"handoff       id {rec.get('id')}   phase {rec.get('phase')}   live {live_side(rec)}   "
                   f"{int((rec.get('deadline', 0) - time.time()) / 60)}m left")
    sections = [
        (f"journal — the {janchor} and every line after it "
         f"({jread // 1024 or 1} kB read of {journal_for(target)}, never the whole file)", journal),
        (f"board — {repo}, unfinished rows only", board_open(repo)),
        ("land — lanes and the landing queue", lanes()),
        (f"workers — live loops under {repo}, last line of each", live_workers(repo)),
        ("open PRs", prs(repo)),
        ("scope — open asks from the owner", out([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-scope"), "count", repo])),
        ("who", "\n".join(who)),
    ]
    print(f"cc-handoff --brief {target}  ({time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())})")
    for title, body in sections:
        print(f"\n== {title} ==")
        print(body.rstrip() if body.strip() else "(none)")
    print(f"\nThat is the whole packet. What is left is what no file holds: ask your predecessor ONE batch of "
          f"questions about it, and run `cc-handoff --ready` when they are answered.")
    return 0


# ---- selfcheck -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def selfcheck():
    os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK", None)   # the suite exports it for ITS fixtures; every start() here is stubbed and the kick cases need the spawn
    nlo = os.environ.pop("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", None)   # the harness switch reroutes alert(); these cases assert the REAL path via the stubbed run()
    import tempfile
    global HANDOFF, RECORDS, STATE, DEV, win_id, pane_live, pane_live_id, tmux, run, out, notify, alert, self_wid, age
    real_alert = alert
    real_out = out
    p = f = 0

    def ok(what, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
            print(f"  ✓ {what}")
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  ✗ {what}")

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
        HANDOFF = os.path.join(d, "handoff")
        RECORDS = os.path.join(d, "context")
        STATE = os.path.join(d, "state")               # derive/retire touch spool dirs under it: never the real one
        # nothing real is started, killed or pushed from a selfcheck: tmux and the shell-outs are stubbed
        wins, msgs, dead, me, tcalls, panes = {"r1": "@1"}, [], set(), [""], [], {}

        def win_id(n):
            return wins.get(n)

        def pane_live(n):
            return n in wins and n not in dead      # `dead` = the window is still there, the session in it is not

        def pane_live_id(wid):
            return any(i == wid and n not in dead for n, i in wins.items())

        ages = {}

        def age(pid):
            return ages.get(pid, 0)                 # the orphan stub hands the window id over as the pane pid

        def self_wid():
            return me[0]                            # the window --retire is pretending to run in

        def tmux(*a, _log=True, **k):
            if _log:
                tcalls.append(a)
            if ";" in a:                            # `cmd … ; cmd …`: one call, run in order
                i = a.index(";")
                tmux(*a[:i], _log=False); tmux(*a[i + 1:], _log=False)
                return ""
            if a[0] == "list-windows":
                if "pane_start_command" in " ".join(a):   # lanes(): name, id, pane pid, the command it was made with
                    return "\n".join("\t".join([n, i, "4242", f"cc-land {n[4:]} --wait"]) for n, i in wins.items())
                if "pane_pid" in " ".join(a):       # orphans(): id, "pid", name
                    return "\n".join(f"{i} {i} {n}" for n, i in wins.items())
                return "\n".join(wins.values())
            if a[0] == "capture-pane":
                return shown(a[-1])
            if a[0] == "new-window":
                wins[a[a.index("-n") + 1]] = "@%d" % (len(wins) + 1)
            elif a[0] == "kill-window":
                for n, i in list(wins.items()):
                    if i == a[-1]:
                        del wins[n]
            elif a[0] == "rename-window":
                for n, i in list(wins.items()):
                    if i == a[a.index("-t") + 1]:
                        wins[a[-1]] = wins.pop(n)
            return ""

        # the fake TUI: a pane is a string, or a callable drawing the screen of the moment. `tui` is its
        # keyboard log — when accept-dialog was asked per window, and what was typed where, and when.
        BOX = '╭──────────────────────╮\n│ > Try "fix the bug"   │\n╰──────────────────────╯'
        IDLE = BOX + "\n  " + IDLE_HINT
        DIALOG = ("WARNING: Loading development channels can be unsafe.\n ❯ 1. I am using this for local "
                  "development\n   2. Exit\n Enter to confirm · Esc to cancel")
        tui = {"asked": {}, "typed": []}

        def shown(wid):
            v = panes.get(wid, "")
            return v() if callable(v) else v

        def run(argv, wait=True):
            msgs.append(list(argv))
            if argv[0].endswith("cc-slack") and argv[1:2] == ["accept-dialog"]:
                tui["asked"][argv[2]] = time.time()
            if argv[0].endswith("cc-msg"):        # the keyboard: keys reach whatever is in FRONT of the pane, so
                wid = wins.get(argv[1])           # the stub fails where cc-msg would not type — no window, or a
                                                  # dialog it may not answer (the real one spools there; a handoff
                                                  # that cannot type NOW is what these cases are about)
                if wid is None or any(x in shown(wid) for x in ("Enter to confirm", "development channels")):
                    return False
                tui["typed"].append((wid, argv[2], time.time()))
                if not callable(panes.get(wid)):  # a static screen: draw the taken turn the way the TUI does
                    panes[wid] = shown(wid) + "\n> " + argv[2] + "\n\n✻ Thinking… (" + WORKING + ")\n" + BOX
            return True

        def notify(t, m):
            msgs.append(["notify", t, m])

        def alert(m):                              # the real one is gated on the caller's env; its shape is kept
            msgs.append([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-slack"), "post", "-c", "#alerts", m])

        ok("a target with a track slugs to one flat file",
           os.path.basename(path("r1/t1")) == "r1--t1.json" and os.path.basename(path("r1")) == "r1.json")
        ok("a target cannot escape the record dir", not TARGET_RE.match("../etc")
           and not TARGET_RE.match("r1/t1/x") and TARGET_RE.match("r1/t1"))

        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)
        role0 = os.environ.pop("CC_ROLE", None)   # …and neither may the ROLE of whoever is running them: these
                                                  # gates are run from a --go worker as often as from a session,
                                                  # and `worker()` would then refuse every start below. Same trap
                                                  # as $CC_HANDOFF above, restored at the end of the section.
        ok("no record at all -> nothing is open", read("r1") is None and status("r1") == 1)
        ok("an overlap needs a live window", start("nosuch") == 1)
        ok("an overlap starts on a live window", start("r1", pct=41.2) == 0)
        rec = read("r1")
        ok("the record names the predecessor as live", rec and rec["live"] == "predecessor")
        ok("the deadline is bounded", rec["deadline"] - rec["started"] == DEFAULT_MAX)
        ok("a second overlap for the same target is refused", start("r1") == 1)
        nw = [a for a in tcalls if a[0] == "new-window"][-1]
        ok("the successor is told ITS OWN window name, so it accepts the startup dialog in its own pane and "
           "not in its predecessor's — one parked on that confirmation until a human noticed (2026-09-01)",
           "CC_HANDOFF_WINDOW='r1~next' " in nw[-1] and nw[nw.index("-n") + 1] == "r1~next")
        # Hermetic on purpose: the CALLER's $HOME and $CC_CTX_RECORDS are exactly what this case is about, so
        # neither may decide it — selftest.sh sets the latter, and the case passed alone and failed in the run.
        env0 = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in ("HOME", "CC_CTX_RECORDS")}
        os.environ.pop("CC_CTX_RECORDS", None)
        os.environ["HOME"] = os.path.join(d, "fakehome")
        got = [handoff_log()]
        os.environ["CC_CTX_RECORDS"] = RECORDS
        got.append(handoff_log())
        ok("a detached child's log follows $HOME, and $CC_CTX_RECORDS ahead of it — a fixture leaves no line "
           "in the box's real handoff.log",
           got == [os.path.join(d, "fakehome", ".cc", "state", "context", "handoff.log"),
                   os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoff.log")])
        for k, v in env0.items():
            os.environ.pop(k, None) if v is None else os.environ.__setitem__(k, v)

        ok("every prompt this file types into a session leads with the machine tag — the kick, the retirement "
           "message and --replace's — so the ask ledger skips them by the tag and never by their wording",
           all(p.startswith(MACHINE) for p in (KICK, RETIRE_MSG, REPLACE_MSG)))

        # ---- the successor must be BRIEFED, and the brief SEEN to land. The brief itself rides in the system
        # prompt, which nothing acts on by itself; on 2026-09-01 a successor sat blank for 25 min because the
        # startup dialog ate the prompt typed at it and nobody read the pane back.
        env1 = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in ("CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT", "CC_CTX_RECORDS", "HOME", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY",
                                               "CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK")}
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT"] = "0"        # no naps, and no waiting on a window that will not come
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK", None)      # the suite sets it for ITS fixtures; these cases are about the kick
        os.environ["CC_CTX_RECORDS"] = RECORDS          # ...and the detached child's log line stays in here
        os.environ["HOME"] = os.path.join(d, "fakehome")   # ...as does the journal line kick_lost() leaves
        ok("start() leaves a detached child to brief the successor",
           any(isinstance(a, list) and a[-3:-1] == ["--kick", "r1"] for a in msgs))
        ok("...and the successor's pane shell EXECS cc, so claude is the pane's direct child — where cc-msg looks "
           "(forked, it was a grandchild and every automatic kick was refused)",
           re.search(r" exec \S*cc __runnext ", nw[-1]) and not nw[-1].endswith("exec bash"))
        nid = wins["r1~next"]
        panes[nid] = IDLE
        ok("a successor at its prompt gets its opening prompt — a brief in the system prompt starts no turn",
           kick("r1", "abc") == 0)
        last = [a for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[0].endswith("cc-msg")][-1]
        ok("...typed into r1~next, ITS window, naming what it is the successor of",
           last[1] == "r1~next" and "SUCCESSOR for r1" in last[2])
        ok("...and leading with the machine tag, so the successor does not spend its first turn declining an "
           "owner row about its own startup (a121, a126, a131)", last[2].startswith(MACHINE))
        t = last[2]
        ok("...and landing is checked by reading the pane BACK", landed(nid, t))
        wrap = "\n".join(t[i:i + 17] for i in range(0, len(t), 17))   # the TUI wraps at the pane width
        panes[nid] = "> " + wrap + "\n\n⏺ Read(progress.md)\n" + IDLE
        ok("...a wrapped echo above the successor's first tool line counts as landed", landed(nid, t))
        panes[nid] = "│ > " + t[:40] + "\n│   " + t[40:80] + "\n  " + IDLE_HINT
        ok("...but the text still SITTING in the input box does not — Enter was not taken (03:32Z's successor)",
           not landed(nid, t))
        panes[nid] = "Welcome to Claude Code\n" + IDLE
        ok("...and a pane that never took it does not pass as landed", not landed(nid, t))
        ok("the child's one log line stays inside the fixture — no test writes real state",
           os.path.exists(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoff.log")))

        posts = lambda: sum(1 for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[1:4] == ["post", "-c", "#alerts"])
        typed = lambda: sum(1 for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[0].endswith("cc-msg"))
        told0, typed0 = posts(), typed()
        panes[nid] = "Do you trust the files in this folder?\n  Enter to confirm · Esc to cancel"
        ok("a prompt is never typed at an open dialog — that is the dialog eating it, again",
           kick("r1", "abc") == 1 and typed() == typed0)
        ok("...cc-slack is asked to press through THAT window first",
           any(isinstance(a, list) and a[:3] == [os.path.join(BIN, "cc-slack"), "accept-dialog", "r1~next"]
               for a in msgs))
        ok("...and a successor left blank goes to #alerts, not left silent beside a live session",
           posts() == told0 + 1)
        jp = os.path.join(d, "fakehome", ".cc", "state", "r1", "planning-handoff.md")
        ok("...and into the target's journal, where the next session reads first",
           os.path.exists(jp) and "sitting blank in main:r1~next" in open(jp).read())
        panes[nid] = lambda: ""                          # live, and never draws a thing
        told0, typed0 = posts(), typed()
        ok("a pane that never drew a prompt is NOT a dialog: one try at the deadline, judged by the pane — "
           "which took nothing, so the owner hears", kick("r1", "abc") == 1 and typed() == typed0 + 1 and posts() == told0 + 1)

        # THE LATE DIALOG, on the real loop with a fake clock: the pane is live from the first instant (the
        # shell forked cc), Claude Code draws the dialog seconds later, and it clears only once pressed.
        real_t, real_s = time.time, time.sleep
        clk = [real_t()]
        time.time = lambda: clk[0]
        # a sub-second sleep is subprocess's own wait poll (cfg() shells out to cc-config): it sleeps for real and
        # leaves the clock alone — on the fake clock it spun the child's whole run into minutes (flake, 2026-09-02)
        time.sleep = lambda n: real_s(n) if 0 < n < 1 else clk.__setitem__(0, clk[0] + (n or 1))
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT"] = "60"
        tui["asked"].pop("r1~next", None)                # the earlier dialog's press is not this one's
        t0 = clk[0]

        def late():
            if clk[0] - t0 < 4:
                return ""                                        # booting: live, nothing drawn yet
            asked = tui["asked"].get("r1~next")
            if asked is None or clk[0] < asked + 3:
                return DIALOG                                    # up until cc-slack presses it through
            turns = [x for x in tui["typed"] if x[0] == nid and x[2] >= asked + 3]
            return IDLE if not turns else "> " + turns[-1][1] + "\n\n✻ Thinking… (" + WORKING + ")\n" + BOX

        panes[nid] = late
        n0, typed0 = len(tui["typed"]), typed()
        acc0 = sum(1 for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[1:3] == ["accept-dialog", "r1~next"])
        rc = kick("r1", "abc")
        sent = tui["typed"][n0:]
        ok("a dialog that draws LATE is waited out: the prompt is typed once, after it has shown AND cleared",
           rc == 0 and len(sent) == 1 and typed() == typed0 + 1
           and sent[0][2] >= tui["asked"]["r1~next"] + 3 and clk[0] - t0 < 60)
        ok("...and cc-slack was asked to press it through, once",
           sum(1 for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[1:3] == ["accept-dialog", "r1~next"]) == acc0 + 1)
        tui["asked"].pop("r1~next"); t0 = clk[0]
        panes[nid] = lambda: "" if clk[0] - t0 < 4 else DIALOG   # a dialog nobody's Enter ever clears
        n0, told0 = len(tui["typed"]), posts()
        ok("...a dialog that never clears is never typed at, and the owner hears within the wait",
           kick("r1", "abc") == 1 and len(tui["typed"]) == n0 and posts() == told0 + 1 and clk[0] - t0 <= 62)
        time.time, time.sleep = real_t, real_s

        # the real alert() and its gate: test state stops at the log (the Slack layer applies no such rule)
        os.environ.pop("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", None)
        n0 = posts(); real_alert("🔁 _cctest99/hx: handover"); real_alert("🔁 _selfcheck/hx: handover")
        ok("alert() never posts a synthetic _cctest/_selfcheck name to the box's #alerts", posts() == n0)
        os.environ["CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY"] = "1"; real_alert("🔁 realrepo: handover")
        ok("...nor anything with CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY set, which every suite sets", posts() == n0)
        os.environ.pop("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", None); real_alert("🔁 realrepo: handover")
        ok("...while a real handover's line goes through", posts() == n0 + 1)
        for k, v in env1.items():
            os.environ.pop(k, None) if v is None else os.environ.__setitem__(k, v)

        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = "wrong-id"
        ok("--ready from a session whose CC_HANDOFF does not match is refused", ready("r1") == 1)
        ok("...and the predecessor is still live", read("r1")["live"] == "predecessor")

        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = rec["id"]
        ok("--ready with no target finds the record by CC_HANDOFF", by_id(rec["id"])["target"] == "r1")
        ok("the successor cuts over", ready() == 0)
        ok("the commit point moved ownership forward", read("r1")["live"] == "successor")
        ok("the predecessor was told to journal and exit",
           any("cc-msg" in a[0] for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list)))
        ok("--ready is idempotent, never a rollback", ready() == 0 and read("r1")["live"] == "successor")
        ok("--abandon after cutover is refused", abandon("r1") == 1 and read("r1")["live"] == "successor")

        lines = [json.loads(l) for l in open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl"))]
        ok("one ledger line per phase, and never two",
           [l["kind"] for l in lines] == ["overlap-started", "overlap-cutover"])

        # a successor that dies before --ready: ownership never moved, so the predecessor never noticed
        clear("r1")
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)
        wins.pop("r1~next", None)
        wins["r1"] = "@1"
        ok("a fresh overlap opens once the last one is cleared", start("r1") == 0)
        wins.pop("r1~next", None)                       # the successor's window is gone
        ok("successor dead before --ready -> the predecessor is still live",
           read("r1")["live"] == "predecessor" and ready("r1", forced=True) == 2)
        ok("--due before the deadline does nothing", due("r1") == 0 and read("r1") is not None)

        r = read("r1")
        r["deadline"] = time.time() - 1
        write(r)
        told1 = sum(1 for m in msgs if m and m[0] == "notify")     # a count, not a total: the cases above tell the owner too
        ok("--due past the deadline below the ceiling abandons", due("r1") == 0 and read("r1") is None)
        ok("the owner heard it once", sum(1 for m in msgs if m and m[0] == "notify") == told1 + 1)
        ok("--due on a cleared record is a no-op (never twice)", due("r1") == 0
           and sum(1 for m in msgs if m and m[0] == "notify") == told1 + 1)

        # at the ceiling with no successor: fall back INTO today's stop-then-restart path
        wins["r1/t1"] = "@2"
        start("r1/t1")
        r = read("r1/t1")
        r["deadline"] = time.time() - 1
        write(r)
        wins.pop("r1/t1~next", None)
        due("r1/t1", over_ceiling=True)
        ok("at the ceiling with no successor, the old path runs — and it is OURS now, not a call back into cc",
           any(isinstance(a, list) and a[-2:] == ["--replace", "r1/t1"] for a in msgs))
        ok("...and the record is cleared, so nothing stays gated", read("r1/t1") is None)

        # ---- retirement DRAINS the predecessor, then ENDS it. A session cannot exit itself, so the drain
        # expires into a KILL; and the names move at cutover, so an owner attaching lands on the live one.
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE"] = "0"
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN"] = "0"

        def cutover(t):
            """a target at the moment after the commit point: predecessor alive, successor waiting"""
            wins[t] = "@" + t
            start(t)
            os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = read(t)["id"]
            ready(t)
            return read(t)

        def decide(payload):
            """what --relay hands the hook back for this payload: the decision dict, or None for nothing"""
            import io, contextlib
            buf = io.StringIO()
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
                relay(payload)
            return json.loads(buf.getvalue()) if buf.getvalue().strip() else None

        relays = lambda: [a for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[0].endswith("cc-msg") and a[2].startswith(RELAY_PREFIX)]

        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)
        wins["r2"] = "@r2"
        ok("--retire with no record kills nothing", retire("r2") == 0 and wins.get("r2") == "@r2")
        start("r2")
        ok("--retire BEFORE cutover kills nothing", retire("r2") == 0 and wins.get("r2") == "@r2"
           and read("r2") is not None)
        me[0] = "@r2"
        ok("...and a prompt into the predecessor before cutover is nobody's to relay",
           decide({"prompt": "early", "session_id": ""}) is None and not relays())
        me[0] = ""
        clear("r2")
        del wins["r2~next"]

        r = cutover("r2")
        pre, suc = r["predecessor"]["tmux"], r["successor"]["tmux"]
        ok("AT CUTOVER the predecessor is `<target>~old` and the successor holds `<target>` — an owner attaching "
           "lands on the live one from the first instant",
           wins.get("r2~old") == pre and wins.get("r2") == suc and "r2~next" not in wins)
        ok("...and the record names the windows they hold now",
           r["predecessor"]["window"] == "r2~old" and r["successor"]["window"] == "r2")
        ren = [a for a in tcalls if a[0] == "rename-window" and "r2~old" in a]
        ok("...both names move in ONE tmux call — no instant with no `<target>` window for a `cc <repo>` to fill",
           len(ren) == 1 and ren[0].count("rename-window") == 2 and ren[0][-1] == "r2")
        told = [a for a in msgs if isinstance(a, list) and a[0].endswith("cc-msg") and a[1] == "r2~old"]
        ok("...the predecessor is told, in ITS window, that it is retiring and that typing here is relayed",
           told and "retiring" in told[-1][2] and "relayed" in told[-1][2])
        ok("...and it too leads with the machine tag: the retiring session's stop message opened an owner row "
           "as well (a132)", told and told[-1][2].startswith(MACHINE))
        ok("a predecessor still alive past the drain is ENDED, and only the successor holds the name",
           retire("r2") == 0 and wins.get("r2") == suc and "r2~old" not in wins and "r2~next" not in wins)
        ok("...the record is cleared afterwards, so nothing stays gated", read("r2") is None)
        ok("...and the completed retirement is remembered where clear() cannot reach it (2026-08-31: a "
           "finished handoff gated the only session left)", os.path.exists(done_path(r["id"])))
        marked = done_path(r["id"])
        ok("one ledger line for the retirement",
           [json.loads(l)["kind"] for l in open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl"))][-1] == "overlap-retired")

        r = cutover("r3")
        dead.add("r3~old")                              # /exit was typed: the window is a bare shell now
        ok("a predecessor that already exited still ends, renames and clears",
           retire("r3") == 0 and wins.get("r3") == r["successor"]["tmux"] and "r3~old" not in wins
           and read("r3") is None)

        # kick() spools the opening prompt under the STARTUP name; cutover moves it to the live name, and
        # retirement removes mail addressed to the dead ~old window (review-157b: both were stranded forever).
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~next"), exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~next", "inbox.spool"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write('{"text":"opening prompt"}\n')
        r = cutover("r9")
        ok("cutover moves the ~next spool to the live name — the opening prompt is not stranded by the rename",
           not os.path.exists(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~next"))
           and open(os.path.join(STATE, "r9", "inbox.spool")).read() == '{"text":"opening prompt"}\n')
        # ...and it takes cc-msg's own lock while it does it: the 60 s drain rewrites that same file as tail+mv,
        # so an append the drain did not see is replaced by its mv — and the rmtree then deletes the only copy.
        held, _fl = [], fcntl.flock
        fcntl.flock = lambda fh, op: held.append((getattr(fh, "name", ""), op,
                                                  os.path.exists(os.path.join(STATE, "r14", "inbox.spool"))))
        try:
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE, "r14~next"), exist_ok=True)
            with open(os.path.join(STATE, "r14~next", "inbox.spool"), "w") as fh:
                fh.write('{"text":"opening prompt"}\n')
            cutover("r14")
        finally:
            fcntl.flock = _fl
        ok("...holding cc-msg's own .inbox.lock, taken before a byte is written and released after the ~next dir "
           "is gone — outside it the drain's mv wins and the moved prompt is deleted with the dir it came from",
           held[:1] == [(os.path.join(STATE, "r14", ".inbox.lock"), fcntl.LOCK_EX, False)]
           and open(os.path.join(STATE, "r14", "inbox.spool")).read() == '{"text":"opening prompt"}\n'
           and not os.path.exists(os.path.join(STATE, "r14~next")))

        os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~old"), exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~old", "inbox.spool"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write('{"text":"retire msg"}\n')
        ok("...and retirement leaves no ~old junk: mail addressed to the dead window dies with it",
           retire("r9") == 0 and not os.path.exists(os.path.join(STATE, "r9~old")))

        r = cutover("r4")
        me[0] = r["predecessor"]["tmux"]                # --retire called from the very window it would kill
        ok("--retire never kills the pane it is called from",
           retire("r4") == 1 and wins.get("r4~old") == r["predecessor"]["tmux"] and read("r4") is not None)
        ok("...and a refused kill leaves no completion marker: the successor stays gated",
           not os.path.exists(done_path(r["id"])))
        ok("...the drain is over, so a prompt typed there is REFUSED with the pointer, not relayed",
           read("r4")["phase"] == "retired" and (decide({"prompt": "hello?", "session_id": ""}) or {}).get("decision")
           == "block" and "retired — use window r4" in decide({"prompt": "hello?"})["reason"] and not relays())
        ok("...but /exit still reaches it: that is how a human ends a TUI", decide({"prompt": "/exit"}) is None)
        me[0] = ""
        r["predecessor"]["tmux"] = r["successor"]["tmux"]   # the record names one window on both sides
        write(r)
        ok("a record naming the same window on both sides kills nothing", retire("r4") == 1
           and wins.get("r4") == r["successor"]["tmux"] and read("r4") is not None)
        r["predecessor"]["tmux"] = "@somebody-else"     # the record names a window that is not there
        write(r)
        ok("a record naming a window that is not there kills nothing — whatever holds `~old` is left alone",
           retire("r4") == 0 and wins.get("r4~old") == "@r4" and wins.get("r4") == r["successor"]["tmux"])
        wins.pop("r4~old")

        r = cutover("r6")
        wins["r6-renamed"] = wins.pop("r6~old")          # its window no longer carries the retiring name
        ok("a predecessor whose window was renamed by hand is still ended, by the id the record names",
           retire("r6") == 0 and r["predecessor"]["tmux"] not in wins.values()
           and wins.get("r6") == r["successor"]["tmux"] and read("r6") is None)

        wins["r13"] = "@r13"
        start("r13")
        rr = read("r13"); nid = rr["successor"]["tmux"]; rr["successor"]["tmux"] = ""; write(rr)
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = rr["id"]
        ok("a record with no successor id still renames it at cutover, by its `~next` name — and learns the id",
           ready("r13") == 0 and wins.get("r13") == nid and "r13~next" not in wins
           and read("r13")["successor"]["tmux"] == nid)
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)
        clear("r13"); wins.pop("r13~old", None); wins.pop("r13", None)

        r = cutover("r5")
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL"] = "0"         # every marker is now older than the TTL
        retire("r5")
        ok("a completion marker is pruned once no session can still be holding that id",
           os.path.exists(done_path(r["id"])) and not os.path.exists(marked))
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL", None)

        # ---- THE DRAIN: what reaches the predecessor between cutover and its end goes to the successor.
        # 02:2xZ, 2026-09-01: the owner's question, typed a minute before the kill, was seen by nobody.
        env2 = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in ("HOME", "CC_CTX_RECORDS")}
        os.environ["HOME"] = os.path.join(d, "fakehome")
        os.environ["CC_CTX_RECORDS"] = RECORDS
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN"] = "600"
        r = cutover("r7")
        pre, suc = r["predecessor"]["tmux"], r["successor"]["tmux"]
        me[0] = pre                                     # the hook runs in the predecessor's pane
        panes[suc] = IDLE
        n0 = len(relays())
        dec = decide({"prompt": "where did the PR land?", "session_id": ""})
        ok("DURING THE DRAIN a prompt typed into the retiring session reaches the successor's window — "
           "`<target>`, which the successor holds — marked as relayed",
           len(relays()) == n0 + 1 and relays()[-1][1] == "r7"
